1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,740
Okay, next section is the stomach.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:04,360
The first disorder is called gastroschisis.

3
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:08,280
Gastro means, refers to the stomach, and schisis means to split,

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,300
so it's as if there's splitting of the stomach.

5
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:13,280
Now, we don't literally get splitting of the stomach in this disorder.

6
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:16,700
Instead, we get a congenital malformation of the abdominal wall,

7
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:20,100
so that it is the abdominal wall that is actually kind of split,

8
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:23,180
which then leads to exposure of the abdominal contents.

9
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:25,660
Let me remind you that when we form the abdominal wall,

10
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:27,600
we actually have a bunch of folds that come together.

11
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:30,260
So there's an anterior fold, there's a posterior fold,

12
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:31,900
and then there's a couple lateral folds,

13
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:35,940
and all of these have to come together to form that anterior abdominal wall.

14
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:39,660
Now, in this particular case, there is a congenital malformation,

15
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:40,920
so you don't form the wall.

16
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:42,780
When you don't form the wall, of course,

17
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:45,120
there's going to be exposure of the abdominal contents.

18
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:47,800
And this is what gastroschisis looks like.

19
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:50,060
You've got a hole in the abdominal wall,

20
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:52,740
which, again, exposes the gastric contents

21
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:54,980
so that you can clearly see the intestine.

22
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:57,620
You're basically directly looking at the intestines.

23
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,460
The next disorder is called omphalocele.

24
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:04,440
And in this particular case, what happens is you get persistent herniation of the bowel

25
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:05,560
into the umbilical cord.

26
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:08,160
Let's go back to development to remind ourselves that

27
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,820
because there was very little space in the abdomen

28
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:14,460
during the early development of the bowel,

29
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:18,220
you actually get some herniation of the bowel into the umbilical cord.

30
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:22,820
And then remember that the bowel undergoes a 90-degree counterclockwise rotation.

31
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:27,600
And when that rotation occurs, it pulls the bowel back out of the umbilical cord

32
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,280
into the body cavity.

33
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:34,960
So in this particular case, in an omphalocele, you have persistent herniation.

34
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:38,920
Now, remember, the herniation was part of development, but it persists.

35
00:01:39,260 --> 00:01:42,300
So you get persistent herniation of the bowel into the umbilical cord.

36
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:45,460
Again, this is due to a failure of the herniated intestines

37
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:48,420
to return to the body cavity during development.

38
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:53,180
Now, interestingly, because you've got this herniation into the umbilical cord,

39
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:55,920
you're going to see the contents of the abdomen.

40
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:59,980
However, it's going to be covered by peritoneum and the amnion of the umbilical cord.

41
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:01,180
So it looks like this.

42
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:04,900
You see the abdominal contents, but they're present in a bubble.

43
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:09,540
And, of course, that bubble is the peritoneum and the amnion of the umbilical cord.

44
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:14,760
So this is very important because it makes for a very clear distinction between gastroschesis.

45
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:18,880
So in gastroschesis, you have a hole in the anterior abdominal cavity,

46
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:21,420
or in the, sorry, you have a hole in the abdominal wall

47
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:24,020
that then reveals the abdominal contents,

48
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:26,940
whereas in an omphalocele, you have herniation of the bowel,

49
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:30,040
which also can reveal the abdominal contents.

50
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:31,880
However, you wouldn't directly see the contents.

51
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:35,240
It would be covered by this bubble-like surface,

52
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,160
which, again, represents the peritoneum and the amnion of the umbilical cord.

53
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,820
So this is a very important distinction to make here.

54
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:46,080
Pyloric stenosis is another congenital disorder.

55
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,260
This is where you get hypertrophy of the pyloric smooth muscle.

56
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:50,480
It's more common in males.

57
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:51,620
Here's the idea.

58
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:53,460
Of course, you've got the esophagus,

59
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:55,800
and then you've got the stomach coming off the esophagus,

60
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:59,760
and eventually you recognize that the stomach feeds into the duodenum,

61
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:03,080
and in order for food to go from the stomach into the duodenum,

62
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:06,340
it's got to cross this sphincter called the pyloric sphincter.

63
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:11,660
Now, what happens in pyloric stenosis is that you get thickening of the wall of the sphincter

64
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:16,820
because of hypertrophy of smooth muscle, which then creates a stenosis,

65
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:19,680
and a stenosis is basically just a tightening of that window,

66
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:23,660
so that now, because you've got tight stenosis here,

67
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:25,880
you can't get the food properly out of the stomach.

68
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:30,480
Now, what's important to note and what's interesting is that these babies,

69
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,340
when they're born, they're actually normal.

70
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:36,140
So there is no pyloric stenosis at birth, and that's actually high yield.

71
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:40,340
The pyloric stenosis develops about two weeks after birth,

72
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:41,880
and that's when it classically presents.

73
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:43,220
So why don't I put it this way?

74
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:45,500
It takes time for the stenosis to develop,

75
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:48,520
and then the patients will present two weeks after birth.

76
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:49,660
Now, how will they present?

77
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:51,140
Well, as they bring food in here,

78
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:53,720
it's not going to be able to pass through this area,

79
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:55,880
and that's going to build up pressure in the stomach.

80
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:58,520
And so eventually, as the pressure continues to build,

81
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:01,160
it's then going to result in the shooting of food this way,

82
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:02,980
and so you get projectile vomiting.

83
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:05,160
Another interesting thing that you can see

84
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:07,020
is that when you look at the abdomen of these babies,

85
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:11,160
because there's so much peristalsis of the stomach against this tight block,

86
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:14,160
that peristalsis can actually be visible on the abdomen.

87
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:15,620
When you do physical exam,

88
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:19,440
you can actually feel this area of hypertrophy of muscle,

89
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:21,640
and it feels like an olive-like mass.

90
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:25,400
It's just a tiny little olive in this baby's abdomen.

91
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:26,980
And the way by which you would treat

92
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:29,540
is you would cut away this muscle, which is called myotomy.

93
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,580
And so these are the key clinical features,

94
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:33,020
and they're all based on the anatomy.

95
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:36,200
Again, it presents usually two weeks after birth.

96
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:38,960
So the clinical features are listed here in your text.

97
00:04:39,340 --> 00:04:41,080
The only thing I would like to point out here,

98
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:42,420
in addition to what I've already said,

99
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:45,620
is that when you get that vomiting, the vomiting is non-bilious.

100
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,900
You know, the contents of the abdomen have not yet met the bile,

101
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,340
because that would occur in the duodenum after the pyloric stenosis.

102
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:55,840
And so, just as a picture, very simplistically,

103
00:04:56,260 --> 00:04:58,860
you realize that pyloric stenosis is here,

104
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:02,120
but the bile is coming in down here in the duodenum.

105
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:05,140
So this food that goes up is not going to have bile within it.

106
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:07,140
So that's non-bilious vomiting.

107
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:09,000
Everything else I've already explained,

108
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:11,180
and so you can review this in your text as well.

109
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,920
Acute gastritis is very straightforward.

110
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:16,400
Maybe it'd be best, actually, to take a step back

111
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:19,740
and just do a global picture of gastritis before we move on.

112
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:21,580
So when you think about gastritis,

113
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:24,860
what you should remind yourselves of is that there's two types.

114
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:28,700
There is acute gastritis, and there is chronic gastritis.

115
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:34,260
And acute gastritis is basically a story of burning of the stomach by acid.

116
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:37,660
So when you have acid and it burns the stomach,

117
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:40,420
the surface of the stomach, that's called acute gastritis.

118
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:44,040
Now let's recall that it would basically be a function of one of two things.

119
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:47,920
In acute gastritis, either you're going to have increased acid production,

120
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,700
that's one way by which you might get acute gastritis,

121
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:55,580
or the other way would be that you would have decreased protection of the mucosa.

122
00:05:55,980 --> 00:06:00,200
Remember that the key protective mechanism against burning of the stomach by acid

123
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:02,160
is that you've got a protective mucosa.

124
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:05,380
So you would have decreased protection from the mucosa.

125
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:10,340
All right, so these are the two ways, M-U-C-O-S-A, sorry about that.

126
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:13,400
These are the two ways in which you're going to get acute gastritis.

127
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:16,240
Either there'll be too much acid or there won't be enough protection.

128
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:18,560
And so, of course, if there's an imbalance here,

129
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:23,300
that will result in an acid damage to the mucosa of the stomach.

130
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:25,180
And that's the key thing in acute gastritis.

131
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:30,000
Chronic gastritis, on the other hand, can be subdivided into two types based on etiology.

132
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:34,100
And so the first type is called chronic autoimmune gastritis.

133
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:38,980
And the second type is called chronic H. pylori gastritis.

134
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:40,740
So these are the two types.

135
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:43,580
And we'll discuss these in much more detail as we move forward.

136
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:46,400
I'm just trying to lay down a global picture in your mind.

137
00:06:46,740 --> 00:06:49,740
So again, acute gastritis, acid damage to the mucosa,

138
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:55,420
which would be due to an imbalance between the mucosal protection and the acidic environment.

139
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,660
And then chronic gastritis will be subdivided into two types.

140
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:01,320
There's an autoimmune type, which is the less common type.

141
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:05,680
And then there's an H. pylori induced type, which is obviously the most common type.

142
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:08,760
Now let's start with our discussion of acute gastritis.

143
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:12,340
So I told you that acute gastritis is acidic damage to the mucosa.

144
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:16,320
And there's an imbalance between the mucosal defenses and the acidic environment.

145
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:19,020
Let's very quickly talk about the mucosal defenses.

146
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:21,880
So there are several types of defenses that we have,

147
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:25,220
which are present within the mucosa that protect against acid.

148
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:27,700
So for example, let's say that this is the mucosa.

149
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:29,960
You know that the mucosa is lined by epithelium.

150
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:36,300
Let me remind you from histology that these epithelial cells are also known as foveolar cells.

151
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:40,740
Now the foveolar cells, they produce mucus, and that makes a mucus layer.

152
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:43,340
And that, of course, protects the cells.

153
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:50,200
There's also a ton of bicarb secretion, which then helps to neutralize any acid that might come anywhere near these cells.

154
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:53,120
It's important to have a normal blood supply,

155
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:58,020
because the normal blood supply provides nutrients to this mucosa to keep it alive and healthy.

156
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:01,020
And also the normal blood supply has the ability to pick up acid,

157
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,560
so that if some acid were actually to leak through this barrier,

158
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:07,600
the blood supply could actually take the acid away from that particular region.

159
00:08:08,020 --> 00:08:13,780
And so your defenses, these are your key defenses that protect you against the acidic environment of the stomach.

160
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:19,940
Now, once you understand that, you can then go into a discussion of the risk factors of acute gastritis.

161
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:23,120
So for example, the first risk factor would be a severe burn.

162
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:29,100
That's also called a curling ulcer when the patients get ulceration in the context of a severe burn.

163
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:30,480
Now what's the idea here?

164
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:34,440
Well, when patients get severe burn, one of the complications is hypovolemia.

165
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:37,640
And if you have hypovolemia, there's going to be less blood flow to the stomach.

166
00:08:37,940 --> 00:08:42,840
And if you have less blood flow to the stomach, then the mucosa is not going to receive as much nutrients,

167
00:08:43,060 --> 00:08:48,460
and you're also not going to be able to sweep away the acid with the blood flow, the way I described earlier.

168
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:52,340
So blood flow is essential for that protective barrier,

169
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:56,860
and if you don't have good blood flow, it's going to increase the risk for developing acute gastritis.

170
00:08:56,860 --> 00:09:06,580
Now, another risk factor would be NSAIDs, and the idea here is that prostaglandins that are normally produced by cyclooxygenase of the foveolar cells

171
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:09,520
are essential for creating this protective barrier.

172
00:09:09,880 --> 00:09:15,080
For example, one thing that the prostaglandins do is that they decrease acid production,

173
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:17,640
so that protects the mucosa by decreasing the acid.

174
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:24,280
They also stimulate the ability of the cells to produce mucus and bicarb, so that would be protective.

175
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:32,660
They also increase the blood flow to the mucosal barrier, so that that can increase both the nutrients as well as the ability to sweep away acid.

176
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:36,640
And so, again, without your prostaglandins, you're going to be in big trouble.

177
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:41,120
And so what the NSAIDs do is they knock out the prostaglandins, and when you knock out the prostaglandins,

178
00:09:41,220 --> 00:09:44,440
you're going to lose all of those beneficial effects of the prostaglandins.

179
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:47,380
That will increase the risk for acute gastritis.

180
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:49,820
Heavy alcohol consumption can do it as well.

181
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:51,520
Alcohol is basically a toxin.

182
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:58,020
It will directly damage the mucosa, allowing for the acid to then subsequently damage the stomach.

183
00:09:58,240 --> 00:09:59,420
So that would be another cause.

184
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:04,720
Chemotherapy can also increase the risk for formation of acute gastritis.

185
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:09,620
And the idea here is that when you give chemotherapy, you're knocking out the cells that are turning over,

186
00:10:09,720 --> 00:10:17,000
and if you knock out the cells that are turning over, then you're not going to be able to regenerate new cells in the mucosa of the stomach.

187
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:23,120
And so when you can't regenerate new cells, you're not going to have as good of a mucosal surface to protect against acid.

188
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:29,660
So chemotherapy knocks out your ability to regenerate the cells of the stomach, hence increasing the risk for acute gastritis.

189
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:35,820
Here's one very high-yield cause of acute gastritis, and that is increased intracranial pressure.

190
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:40,020
Examiner's love to go after this, and I'll explain why in a moment.

191
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:45,580
Increased intracranial pressure, when it results in acute gastritis, is also called a Cushing ulcer.

192
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:52,100
And here's the idea, is that when you get increased intracranial pressure, you're going to get increased vagal stimulation.

193
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:57,980
And that increased vagal stimulation is then going to result in increased acetylcholine.

194
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:05,260
And acetylcholine has the ability to bind the parietal cell receptor to increased acid production.

195
00:11:05,560 --> 00:11:10,020
Let's just go back and remind ourselves of a couple of very simple but important points.

196
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:14,860
Remember that the cell in the stomach that makes the acid is called the parietal cell.

197
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:17,520
That's one important point.

198
00:11:18,280 --> 00:11:24,780
And the parietal cell has three important receptors that can help to regulate the amount of acid that's produced.

199
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:30,360
There's an acetylcholine receptor, so acetylcholine can help to produce acid.

200
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:35,060
There's a gastrin receptor, so that gastrin can induce production of acid.

201
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:38,200
And then the third receptor is that there's a histamine receptor.

202
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:40,580
All three of these are high-yield physiology.

203
00:11:41,060 --> 00:11:45,120
So any one of these substances, acetylcholine, gastrin, or histamine,

204
00:11:45,380 --> 00:11:48,560
can actually induce the production of acid by the parietal cells.

205
00:11:48,980 --> 00:11:52,040
So again, now with that in mind, when you go back and read what we say here,

206
00:11:52,040 --> 00:11:55,760
increased intracranial pressure, of course, would increase vagal stimulation,

207
00:11:56,180 --> 00:11:58,300
which would then increase acetylcholine,

208
00:11:58,640 --> 00:12:01,740
which would then increase production of acid by the parietal cells.

209
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,200
If you have increased acid, that's going to increase the risk of having acid damage to the mucosa.

210
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:09,660
And the last example would be shock.

211
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:12,040
And the idea here is that when patients get shocked,

212
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:15,720
they get a severe decrease in their blood flow to vital organs.

213
00:12:15,820 --> 00:12:19,120
And one of those areas where blood flow would decrease would be the stomach.

214
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:22,680
And, of course, a decreased blood flow will then result in an increased risk

215
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:26,320
for development of acute gastritis due to acidic damage.

216
00:12:26,740 --> 00:12:31,140
Now let me remind you that patients who get shock actually get multiple ulcers,

217
00:12:31,260 --> 00:12:32,480
and those are called stress ulcers.

218
00:12:32,620 --> 00:12:36,740
And that's why most patients in the ICU are actually placed on, for example,

219
00:12:36,900 --> 00:12:42,020
proton pump inhibitors to protect against the formation of multiple stress ulcers.

220
00:12:43,020 --> 00:12:47,060
When patients get acidic damage, that's going to result in one of three possibilities.

221
00:12:47,060 --> 00:12:51,100
You can simply just get superficial inflammation due to damage to the epithelium.

222
00:12:51,480 --> 00:12:54,560
You can actually knock out the epithelium, and when you lose the epithelium,

223
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:55,760
that's called an erosion.

224
00:12:56,120 --> 00:13:00,500
Or you can knock out the entire mucosal layer, and that would be called an ulceration.

225
00:13:00,860 --> 00:13:03,640
So, again, when you knock out the epithelium, that's an erosion.

226
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:07,060
But when you knock out the mucosal layer, that is called an ulceration.

227
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:11,740
And so these are the consequences of the acidic damage.

228
00:13:12,680 --> 00:13:16,560
Chronic gastritis, as I've already stated, can be divided into two types.

229
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:18,680
And it's based on the underlying etiology.

230
00:13:18,840 --> 00:13:22,560
And I told you earlier that there's an autoimmune type, and then there's an H. pylori type.

231
00:13:22,980 --> 00:13:27,100
In chronic autoimmune gastritis, you get autoimmune destruction of the parietal cells.

232
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:31,400
Now, recall that the parietal cells are predominantly present in the body and the fundus.

233
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:32,580
So let's go back and review.

234
00:13:32,960 --> 00:13:36,100
Remember that when you think about the stomach, that's the esophagus there.

235
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:39,740
And then you know that the stomach is coming off, and when the stomach comes off,

236
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:40,840
you've got multiple regions.

237
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:43,460
So you've got here, this represents the cardia.

238
00:13:43,900 --> 00:13:47,180
This represents the fundus, which would actually be a little bit higher like that.

239
00:13:47,380 --> 00:13:50,000
And then there's a body here, and then there's an antrum here.

240
00:13:50,260 --> 00:13:52,040
So those are the four regions of the stomach.

241
00:13:52,180 --> 00:13:56,320
The cardia, the fundus, the body, and then the antrum.

242
00:13:56,860 --> 00:14:00,860
Now, it's in the fundus in the body where you predominantly have your parietal cells.

243
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:05,360
And so in chronic autoimmune gastritis, what happens is that you get an autoimmune destruction

244
00:14:05,360 --> 00:14:09,180
of the parietal cells in that particular region, in the body and the fundus.

245
00:14:09,220 --> 00:14:13,520
And this is very high yield to know that in this particular case, it's in the body and the fundus.

246
00:14:14,280 --> 00:14:19,700
Now, this is actually a T-cell mediated damage, so that it's a type 4 hypersensitivity reaction.

247
00:14:20,140 --> 00:14:25,520
But as a consequence of that damage, what you get in the blood is antibodies against the parietal cells

248
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:28,300
or antibodies against intrinsic factor.

249
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:29,660
And I want to make it very clear.

250
00:14:29,780 --> 00:14:31,740
These antibodies are not causing the damage.

251
00:14:32,060 --> 00:14:33,580
They are a side effect of the damage.

252
00:14:33,580 --> 00:14:37,080
They are a consequence of the damage, and they're useful for diagnosis.

253
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:40,940
Now, clinically, what's going to happen is as you begin to knock out the parietal cells,

254
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:43,320
you're going to begin to knock out the thickness of the mucosa.

255
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:46,900
And so the patients are going to get atrophy of the mucosa.

256
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:50,060
So that's going to be one clinical feature that we're going to see.

257
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:53,980
Another clinical feature is that because you're knocking out the parietal cells,

258
00:14:54,060 --> 00:14:56,280
you're knocking out the ability to produce acid.

259
00:14:56,660 --> 00:14:58,860
And so when you knock out the ability to produce acid,

260
00:14:59,140 --> 00:15:02,700
what's going to happen is there's going to be something called achlorhydria.

261
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:07,300
And I've written that here in your notes here, where I say that achlorhydria will develop.

262
00:15:07,420 --> 00:15:08,380
And what is achlorhydria?

263
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:10,740
Low acid production by the stomach.

264
00:15:11,220 --> 00:15:14,520
Now, of course, if there's achlorhydria, you won't have good acid in the stomach,

265
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:18,780
and so the gastrin levels will increase because you've lost the negative feedback.

266
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:22,940
Gastrin is the key molecule that drives the parietal cells to produce acid.

267
00:15:22,940 --> 00:15:27,660
So if there's low acid, there's going to be increased gastrin trying to make extra acid.

268
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:33,540
And that increased gastrin level will also be associated with G-cell hyperplasia in the antrum.

269
00:15:33,820 --> 00:15:38,540
Let me remind you that it's the G-cells that make gastrin, and the G-cells are present in the antrum.

270
00:15:38,940 --> 00:15:43,160
And so if there's going to be increased gastrin, it will be made by the G-cells,

271
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:45,580
and those G-cells will undergo hyperplasia in the antrum.

272
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:48,900
And all of this is high yield because it ties into a little bit of physiology.

273
00:15:48,900 --> 00:15:54,560
Now, the third clinical feature will be that the patients will get megaloblastic anemia.

274
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:56,600
Now, why do they get megaloblastic anemia?

275
00:15:56,780 --> 00:16:01,740
Well, remember that the parietal cells, which are being destroyed in this disorder, they make intrinsic factor.

276
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:06,480
An intrinsic factor is necessary for vitamin B12 absorption.

277
00:16:06,680 --> 00:16:12,240
Remember that intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 in the intestine, and then it goes to the terminal ileum,

278
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:17,140
and the terminal ileum recognizes the vitamin B12 intrinsic factor complex,

279
00:16:17,140 --> 00:16:19,760
which then allows vitamin B12 to come into the body.

280
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:24,800
Well, if the patients have a destruction of their parietal cells, they won't be making intrinsic factor,

281
00:16:24,860 --> 00:16:28,140
and if they're not making intrinsic factor, they can't bring in vitamin B12.

282
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:33,320
And of course, when they don't bring in vitamin B12, that'll result in megaloblastic anemia.

283
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:38,180
Now, that entire paradigm that I've just described is also called pernicious anemia,

284
00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:40,540
again, due to a lack of intrinsic factor.

285
00:16:40,540 --> 00:16:45,040
And actually, this is the most common cause of vitamin B12 deficiency.

286
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:51,700
So, chronic autoimmune gastritis, most common cause of vitamin B12 deficiency.

287
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:56,600
Now, these patients will also have an increased risk for gastric adenocarcinoma.

288
00:16:56,860 --> 00:17:01,600
And the idea here is that the chronic inflammation is going to induce something called intestinal metaplasia.

289
00:17:02,020 --> 00:17:05,540
And when you get intestinal metaplasia, that metaplasia is a pathologic metaplasia,

290
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:08,700
which will have an increased risk for the development of cancer.

291
00:17:08,700 --> 00:17:13,060
Let me take a step back and remind you that normally, if you were to look at the wall of the stomach,

292
00:17:13,280 --> 00:17:14,980
there would be no inflammatory cells.

293
00:17:15,580 --> 00:17:15,800
All right?

294
00:17:15,860 --> 00:17:18,640
So, you would not see any inflammatory cells in the wall of the stomach.

295
00:17:19,140 --> 00:17:22,360
However, if you get chronic inflammation of the stomach,

296
00:17:22,460 --> 00:17:25,580
now you've got a ton of inflammatory cells present in the wall of the stomach.

297
00:17:26,020 --> 00:17:28,800
Well, the cells of the stomach will then undergo metaplasia,

298
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:30,800
because now they're seeing a ton of inflammatory cells,

299
00:17:31,100 --> 00:17:36,680
and they'll start to form the cells that are normally in the presence of a ton of inflammatory cells.

300
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:38,340
And those are the intestinal cells.

301
00:17:38,340 --> 00:17:42,980
Remember that in the intestine, you have pyre's patches with a bunch of inflammatory cells within it,

302
00:17:43,060 --> 00:17:44,740
i.e. a bunch of lymphocytes.

303
00:17:45,080 --> 00:17:47,220
And so, when you get a bunch of lymphocytes in the stomach,

304
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:51,100
you get this metaplasia where the stomach cells become like the intestinal cells.

305
00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:53,780
And so, we call that, again, intestinal metaplasia.

306
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:57,180
Now, here's a classic picture of intestinal metaplasia.

307
00:17:57,420 --> 00:18:01,600
And let me just remind you that from normal histology, these are your foveolar cells.

308
00:18:01,880 --> 00:18:05,320
So, the foveolar cells don't have big circles of mucus within them.

309
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:10,680
And, of course, the foveolar cells are the normal cells that would be lining the surface of the stomach.

310
00:18:11,080 --> 00:18:15,700
Now, here you can see that you've got these goblet cells with big circles of mucus within them.

311
00:18:16,120 --> 00:18:21,180
And so, the presence of those large, big goblets of mucus, those are called goblet cells,

312
00:18:21,280 --> 00:18:23,360
and they indicate intestinal metaplasia.

313
00:18:23,660 --> 00:18:26,520
And this is a very high-yield principle to be aware of.

314
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:30,680
It goes back to a little bit of normal histology, and it goes back to a very important risk,

315
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:33,480
and that is the risk for gastric adenocarcinoma.

316
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:39,560
The second type of chronic gastritis is chronic H. pylori gastritis.

317
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:44,320
And this represents the vast majority of chronic gastritis, about 90% of cases.

318
00:18:44,700 --> 00:18:46,240
Of course, it's due to H. pylori.

319
00:18:46,580 --> 00:18:51,780
The H. pylori bug induces acute and chronic inflammation, which then results in gastritis.

320
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:59,580
The reason why H. pylori can induce acute and chronic inflammation is because it produces ureases and proteases,

321
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:04,280
which then result in inflammation and weakening of the mucosal defenses.

322
00:19:04,700 --> 00:19:07,760
Now, it's important to note that H. pylori does not invade into the stomach.

323
00:19:07,900 --> 00:19:14,420
It simply sits on the surface of the epithelium, but it produces these different byproducts,

324
00:19:14,420 --> 00:19:18,340
which can then result in inflammation and weakening of the mucosal defenses.

325
00:19:18,340 --> 00:19:22,260
The most common site is the antrum, and that's important because, again,

326
00:19:22,320 --> 00:19:26,700
when you think about chronic gastritis, remember there's autoimmune, which involves the fundus in the body,

327
00:19:26,820 --> 00:19:29,460
and then there's H. pylori, which involves the antrum.

328
00:19:29,560 --> 00:19:31,740
So they involve very different regions of the stomach.

329
00:19:32,260 --> 00:19:33,620
Here's a picture of H. pylori.

330
00:19:33,880 --> 00:19:37,400
You can clearly see these little slender organisms.

331
00:19:37,880 --> 00:19:40,120
So this is the classic picture of H. pylori.

332
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:42,260
Remember that they sit on top of the epithelium.

333
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:45,380
They do not invade into the stomach mucosa.

334
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:50,580
The presentation of patients with H. pylori is that they get epigastric abdominal pain,

335
00:19:50,900 --> 00:19:55,300
and there's an increased risk for ulceration, and that's because with all this chronic inflammation,

336
00:19:55,580 --> 00:19:56,620
you can develop an ulcer.

337
00:19:56,900 --> 00:20:00,580
There's an increased risk for gastric adenocarcinoma, and that's because, again,

338
00:20:00,680 --> 00:20:03,920
with this chronic inflammation, you're going to get an intestinal metoplasia,

339
00:20:04,220 --> 00:20:07,000
and the intestinal metoplasia can eventually become cancer.

340
00:20:07,220 --> 00:20:09,360
So there'll be an increased risk for adenocarcinoma.

341
00:20:09,700 --> 00:20:12,300
And the third risk will be an increased risk for malt lymphoma.

342
00:20:12,300 --> 00:20:16,320
Now, when you have all this chronic inflammation, what you're going to do is you're going to generate

343
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:18,700
germinal centers within the gastric wall.

344
00:20:19,080 --> 00:20:21,280
And when you generate germinal centers within the gastric wall,

345
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:24,540
you're also going to generate post-germinal center B cells.

346
00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:28,740
And when you generate post-germinal center B cells, remember that that actually represents

347
00:20:28,740 --> 00:20:33,660
a special zone within your inflammatory infiltrate called the marginal zone.

348
00:20:33,920 --> 00:20:37,900
Remember our discussion of how you can have a follicle, which represents the germinal center,

349
00:20:37,900 --> 00:20:40,880
and then outside of the follicle, you can have something called the mantle,

350
00:20:41,320 --> 00:20:43,880
and then outside of the mantle, you can have a marginal zone.

351
00:20:44,120 --> 00:20:47,180
This goes back to our discussion of white blood cell pathology.

352
00:20:47,540 --> 00:20:52,900
Now, the marginal zone only forms when there is some sort of chronic inflammatory state

353
00:20:53,420 --> 00:21:00,060
or some sort of chronic inflammation, which necessitates the need for a post-germinal center B cell.

354
00:21:00,060 --> 00:21:05,680
So, in this particular case, you've got this chronic inflammation, which then results in production

355
00:21:05,680 --> 00:21:11,680
of these post-germinal center B cells, which then again results in the production of a marginal zone.

356
00:21:12,020 --> 00:21:15,720
And it's this marginal zone that can eventually result in malt lymphoma.

357
00:21:16,100 --> 00:21:20,960
Malt means a mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, and then, of course, it's a lymphoma.

358
00:21:21,240 --> 00:21:25,560
So, this is a B cell lymphoma that can classically be seen within the wall of the stomach.

359
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,840
Now, I've already discussed this in extreme detail when we did the white blood cell pathology lecture.

360
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:32,420
So, you can review it in your text there as well.

361
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:36,600
Now, what's interesting is you've kind of got three complications.

362
00:21:36,820 --> 00:21:39,040
You had ulceration, cancer, and lymphoma.

363
00:21:39,380 --> 00:21:42,260
And it's interesting that triple therapy is what's given,

364
00:21:42,580 --> 00:21:47,200
and the triple therapy actually helps to reduce the risk for the three complications.

365
00:21:47,380 --> 00:21:48,920
Now, that's just coincidental, obviously.

366
00:21:49,260 --> 00:21:54,040
But the point is it helps to remind you of their three complications and the fact that you give triple therapy.

367
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:57,380
Of course, when you give triple therapy, you're going to resolve the gastritis.

368
00:21:57,380 --> 00:22:01,840
And if you resolve the gastritis, the ulcer will resolve, if the patient has ulceration.

369
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:04,960
If the patient had intestinal metaplasia, that will resolve,

370
00:22:05,100 --> 00:22:08,860
because remember from Chapter 1 of our text that metaplasia is reversible.

371
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:14,040
And then, if there was an increased inflammatory infiltrate with a risk of lymphoma,

372
00:22:14,260 --> 00:22:18,360
of course, that inflammatory infiltrate will disappear once the gastritis disappears,

373
00:22:18,680 --> 00:22:20,760
and so the risk of lymphoma will also disappear.

374
00:22:20,760 --> 00:22:24,560
So triple therapy is essential to avoid the complications of H. pylori.

375
00:22:25,240 --> 00:22:28,340
And, of course, it's also essential to simply just resolve the gastritis

376
00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:31,120
in the clinical presentation that's associated with it.

377
00:22:31,380 --> 00:22:34,860
Now, when you give triple therapy, you want to make sure that you've gotten rid of the bug.

378
00:22:35,180 --> 00:22:39,260
And so the way by which you do that is you can look for urea in the breath,

379
00:22:39,500 --> 00:22:41,940
because remember that the bug is making ureases,

380
00:22:42,140 --> 00:22:44,940
and so it produces urea as a byproduct.

381
00:22:44,940 --> 00:22:49,260
And so when there's lack of urea in the breath, that will tell you that the bug has been killed.

382
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:52,060
Another way to check is to look at the stool.

383
00:22:52,520 --> 00:22:55,940
And in the stool, you'll see lack of H. pylori antigen.

384
00:22:56,720 --> 00:22:58,940
So that would also be helpful to confirm eradication.

385
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:03,100
The next discussion is peptic ulcer disease.

386
00:23:03,200 --> 00:23:08,060
And in peptic ulcer disease, the idea here is that you get a solitary ulcer of the mucosa

387
00:23:08,060 --> 00:23:13,260
that either involves the proximal duodenum, 90% of the time it's in the proximal duodenum,

388
00:23:13,260 --> 00:23:15,100
or in the distal stomach.

389
00:23:15,740 --> 00:23:19,420
Now, when you get a duodenal ulcer, it's almost always due to H. pylori.

390
00:23:19,520 --> 00:23:22,680
That is the number one way by which duodenal ulcers can form.

391
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:25,400
However, rarely, it can also be due to ZE syndrome.

392
00:23:26,540 --> 00:23:29,760
Remember that ZE syndrome is when a patient has a gastronoma.

393
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:33,640
The gastronoma is a tumor that produces excess gastrin.

394
00:23:33,960 --> 00:23:35,320
The gastrin goes into the blood.

395
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:39,420
It hits the parietal cells, and the parietal cells then begin to secrete massive amounts of acid.

396
00:23:39,420 --> 00:23:45,240
And the production of massive amounts of acid can result in a peptic ulcer disease with a duodenal ulcer.

397
00:23:45,620 --> 00:23:47,960
And in ZE syndrome, you can also have multiple ulcers.

398
00:23:48,100 --> 00:23:50,880
You can also have ulcers that extend into the jejunum, etc.

399
00:23:51,200 --> 00:23:54,260
But anyway, that's one way by which a duodenal ulcer can arise.

400
00:23:54,720 --> 00:23:56,400
Now, how is this going to present?

401
00:23:56,400 --> 00:24:04,100
Whether it be due to H. pylori or ZE syndrome, for example, it will present with epigastric pain that improves with meals.

402
00:24:04,480 --> 00:24:12,260
Now, when a patient takes a meal, the duodenum begins to prepare itself for the potential receiving of that acid bolus from the stomach.

403
00:24:12,600 --> 00:24:22,340
And so immediately, the duodenum begins to increase its defenses and produce neutralizing substances that will protect it against the acid that's eventually coming from the stomach.

404
00:24:22,340 --> 00:24:27,860
Those protective mechanisms are also going to improve the pain, and that's why the pain improves with meals.

405
00:24:28,420 --> 00:24:35,780
Now, the way by which you could prove that there's a duodenal ulcer and confirm the etiology is to do endoscopic biopsy.

406
00:24:36,780 --> 00:24:40,540
And that would show the ulcer along with hypertrophy of the Bruner's glands.

407
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:48,800
And the Bruner's glands are glands present within the duodenum, the wall of the duodenum, that, of course, produce a mucus-like substance.

408
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:56,200
And so, of course, there would be excess mucus-like substance to try to protect against this damage that's occurring with a duodenal ulcer.

409
00:24:56,720 --> 00:24:59,740
Now, one of the complications of a duodenal ulcer is that it can rupture.

410
00:25:00,120 --> 00:25:07,660
And this would, one of the complications of rupture is that you can get bleeding from the gastroduodenal artery, or you can get acute pancreatitis.

411
00:25:07,980 --> 00:25:13,220
Why don't I take a step back and remind you that you can have ulceration on the anterior wall or the posterior wall.

412
00:25:13,220 --> 00:25:16,200
The most common site would be the anterior wall.

413
00:25:16,600 --> 00:25:23,880
However, were it to occur along the posterior wall, the complication would be that you could get rupture from the gastroduodenal artery.

414
00:25:24,340 --> 00:25:27,700
That's because that runs at the posterior aspect of the duodenum.

415
00:25:27,880 --> 00:25:35,200
Or you could actually activate the pancreas and the pancreatic enzymes and create an acute pancreatitis.

416
00:25:35,540 --> 00:25:38,980
Now, of course, both of these would be a complication of posterior ulcers.

417
00:25:38,980 --> 00:25:46,000
And I think in your text, it actually says that the gastroduodenal artery bleeding is due to anterior ulcers.

418
00:25:46,300 --> 00:25:55,080
So I would advise you or ask that you correct that in the text at this point and make sure that you make it clear that both of these are a complication of posterior ulcers.

419
00:25:56,480 --> 00:25:59,440
Now, the second type of peptic ulcer disease would be a gastric ulcer.

420
00:25:59,700 --> 00:26:02,640
This is usually due to H. pylori, but not always.

421
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:05,600
And there are other causes, including NSAIDs and bile reflux.

422
00:26:05,600 --> 00:26:13,020
So we could say perhaps like 70% of gastric ulcers due to H. pylori, maybe another 20% is due to NSAIDs.

423
00:26:13,020 --> 00:26:17,800
So the two big causes, H. pylori and NSAIDs, but bile reflux is also an important cause.

424
00:26:18,200 --> 00:26:21,520
This is going to present with epigastric pain, and it worsens with meals.

425
00:26:21,600 --> 00:26:26,000
The moment you take a meal, acid begins to generate within the stomach to digest that food,

426
00:26:26,320 --> 00:26:28,220
and so the pain is going to worsen with a meal.

427
00:26:28,220 --> 00:26:32,180
Now, the ulcer is usually located on the lesser curvature of the antrum,

428
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:35,640
and rupture would carry a risk of bleeding from the left gastric artery.

429
00:26:36,200 --> 00:26:41,360
Remember that the left gastric artery, it runs along the lesser curvature of the antrum,

430
00:26:41,440 --> 00:26:44,600
and so if there's rupture, you can get bleeding from the left gastric artery.

431
00:26:45,580 --> 00:26:50,600
Now, one of the differential diagnoses when you have an ulcer in the GI tract is cancer,

432
00:26:50,780 --> 00:26:52,260
so we always got to think about cancer.

433
00:26:52,260 --> 00:26:55,960
And so when we have peptic ulcer disease, one of the things that comes to mind is,

434
00:26:56,240 --> 00:27:00,480
is this really peptic ulcer disease due to, for example, H. pylori, or is it cancer?

435
00:27:00,980 --> 00:27:05,340
Well, let me start the discussion by saying that duodenal ulcers are almost never malignant,

436
00:27:05,900 --> 00:27:07,540
and so that's a very important principle.

437
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:10,420
In fact, duodenal carcinoma is super, super rare.

438
00:27:10,540 --> 00:27:13,240
In fact, when you think about the cancers that you study in the GI tract,

439
00:27:13,540 --> 00:27:17,100
you study esophageal cancer, you study stomach cancer, you study colon cancer,

440
00:27:17,100 --> 00:27:20,440
you haven't really studied a lot about small bowel cancer,

441
00:27:20,440 --> 00:27:23,160
and that's because duodenal cancer is very, very rare.

442
00:27:23,660 --> 00:27:27,400
So duodenal ulcers are almost never malignant, and they're usually not biopsied.

443
00:27:27,760 --> 00:27:33,120
So we don't necessarily have to worry about that when we think about the differential diagnosis of cancer.

444
00:27:33,540 --> 00:27:35,860
And, of course, most peptic ulcer disease is duodenal.

445
00:27:36,120 --> 00:27:40,200
However, when you have a gastric ulcer, you really do have to worry about cancer,

446
00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:43,980
and that's because gastric cancer can also create ulceration.

447
00:27:44,300 --> 00:27:48,860
And so it's very important to be familiar with the morphology of the ulcer,

448
00:27:48,860 --> 00:27:50,560
whether it be benign or malignant.

449
00:27:50,720 --> 00:27:53,120
So what are features that would suggest that it's benign,

450
00:27:53,200 --> 00:27:55,480
and what are features that would suggest that it's malignant?

451
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:57,960
So this is what a benign ulcer looks like.

452
00:27:58,180 --> 00:28:01,740
And the most important thing about this benign ulcer is that, number one, it's small.

453
00:28:01,900 --> 00:28:03,780
That's a key feature is that the ulcer is small.

454
00:28:04,220 --> 00:28:06,280
Number two, it's punched out.

455
00:28:06,380 --> 00:28:10,320
It looks like somebody took a hole puncher and punched out this hole.

456
00:28:10,320 --> 00:28:14,580
And number three, the margins next to it, when you look at the adjacent tissue next to it,

457
00:28:14,640 --> 00:28:15,720
it's not piling up.

458
00:28:15,720 --> 00:28:17,040
It's just sort of normal.

459
00:28:17,220 --> 00:28:18,280
It's just flat and normal.

460
00:28:18,720 --> 00:28:21,360
And so that's the important feature of a benign ulcer.

461
00:28:22,220 --> 00:28:23,440
Of course, why is this important?

462
00:28:23,540 --> 00:28:27,460
Because if you're the endoscopist and you're going in and taking a look at the stomach via scope,

463
00:28:27,660 --> 00:28:29,900
you're going to look at the ulcer and you're going to try to make a guess

464
00:28:29,900 --> 00:28:31,740
as to whether you think it's benign or malignant.

465
00:28:32,240 --> 00:28:35,920
And so benign features such as a small ulcer that looks punched out

466
00:28:35,920 --> 00:28:39,620
with no heaping up of the mucosa on the edge of the ulcer,

467
00:28:39,700 --> 00:28:42,140
no piling up of the mucosa on the edge of the ulcer,

468
00:28:42,140 --> 00:28:45,480
these are all features that suggest that you're dealing with a benign ulcer.

469
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,800
Now, on the other hand, a malignant ulcer, it looks irregular.

470
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:50,200
It doesn't look punched out.

471
00:28:50,560 --> 00:28:52,680
And on top of that, it's much larger.

472
00:28:52,860 --> 00:28:54,920
It's much bigger than that ulcer that I just showed you.

473
00:28:55,120 --> 00:28:59,880
And on top of that, you can see all this piling up of the mucosa at the edge of the ulcer.

474
00:29:00,180 --> 00:29:03,600
And that also is a feature that's worrisome for malignancy.

475
00:29:03,880 --> 00:29:07,600
Of course, the only way to make the final determination would be to do biopsy.

476
00:29:07,600 --> 00:29:10,440
Now, this brings us to a discussion of gastric cancer.

477
00:29:10,620 --> 00:29:14,560
And so let's remind ourselves that gastric carcinoma is a malignant proliferation

478
00:29:14,560 --> 00:29:16,060
of surface epithelial cells.

479
00:29:16,360 --> 00:29:18,320
Those epithelial cells are columnar cells.

480
00:29:18,480 --> 00:29:20,780
So we're going to get an adenocarcinoma.

481
00:29:21,020 --> 00:29:21,200
All right?

482
00:29:21,320 --> 00:29:24,680
So gastric carcinoma is basically an adenocarcinoma.

483
00:29:25,100 --> 00:29:27,500
Now, you can divide gastric carcinoma into two types.

484
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:29,780
There's an intestinal type and a diffuse type.

485
00:29:30,140 --> 00:29:34,940
In the intestinal type, you get a large irregular ulcer with heaped up margins,

486
00:29:34,940 --> 00:29:36,140
as I've just described.

487
00:29:36,140 --> 00:29:39,700
And this most commonly involves the lesser curvature of the antrum.

488
00:29:40,120 --> 00:29:43,500
Now, remember that the lesser curvature of the antrum is exactly the same place

489
00:29:43,500 --> 00:29:45,900
where we got stomach ulcers and peptic ulcer disease.

490
00:29:46,260 --> 00:29:51,900
And so, again, that's why it's so important to know the differences between benign ulcers

491
00:29:51,900 --> 00:29:56,280
and malignant ulcers and how they would be distinguished morphologically.

492
00:29:57,420 --> 00:30:00,640
The risk factors for the intestinal type include intestinal menoplasia.

493
00:30:01,080 --> 00:30:03,700
Of course, that's very straightforward,

494
00:30:03,700 --> 00:30:06,320
because intestinal menoplasia would give you the intestinal type.

495
00:30:06,600 --> 00:30:07,960
Now, let's go back and remind ourselves,

496
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:10,120
what are things that would give you intestinal menoplasia?

497
00:30:10,520 --> 00:30:13,940
Well, number one, chronic autoimmune gastritis gives you intestinal menoplasia.

498
00:30:14,180 --> 00:30:18,120
And number two, chronic H. pylori gastritis gives you intestinal menoplasia.

499
00:30:18,320 --> 00:30:19,960
So those are the two key risk factors.

500
00:30:20,340 --> 00:30:23,900
Also, an important risk factor is nitrosamines, especially in smoked foods.

501
00:30:23,900 --> 00:30:29,160
So remember from our discussion in the neoplasia chapter that I mentioned about how nitrosamines

502
00:30:29,160 --> 00:30:30,380
are a carcinogen.

503
00:30:30,920 --> 00:30:32,180
They're present in smoked foods.

504
00:30:32,680 --> 00:30:37,040
And they help to explain the increased incidence of stomach cancer in Japan.

505
00:30:37,520 --> 00:30:42,660
And then a final important thing to remember is that patients who have blood type A,

506
00:30:42,900 --> 00:30:46,760
they have an increased risk of developing the intestinal type of stomach cancer.

507
00:30:47,140 --> 00:30:49,660
It's a very strange thing, but you've got to remember it.

508
00:30:49,660 --> 00:30:54,080
And so the way I do that is just remember that when we abbreviate carcinoma, we always just say CA,

509
00:30:54,280 --> 00:30:56,180
like adenos CA or squamous CA.

510
00:30:56,460 --> 00:30:58,420
At least that's the way that we do it as pathologists.

511
00:30:58,820 --> 00:31:04,000
And so that A, maybe that'll help you remember that there's an association of intestinal type

512
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:05,960
of stomach cancer with blood type A.

513
00:31:06,520 --> 00:31:10,680
Now, the second type of stomach cancer, which is much less common but is super important

514
00:31:10,680 --> 00:31:13,380
and super high yield on board exams, is called the diffuse type.

515
00:31:14,040 --> 00:31:18,700
In the diffuse type, what happens is that you get signet ring cells that diffusely infiltrate

516
00:31:18,700 --> 00:31:19,580
the gastric wall.

517
00:31:19,900 --> 00:31:24,100
And when they diffusely infiltrate the gastric wall, that results in cancer plus a reaction

518
00:31:24,100 --> 00:31:25,020
to that cancer.

519
00:31:25,640 --> 00:31:30,100
That reaction is called desmoplasia, which results in a thickening of the stomach wall

520
00:31:30,100 --> 00:31:32,180
creating something called linitis plastica.

521
00:31:32,580 --> 00:31:35,280
Now, there's a little bit of explanation here before we can move forward.

522
00:31:35,520 --> 00:31:37,120
First of all, what's a signet ring cell?

523
00:31:37,200 --> 00:31:41,940
A signet ring cell is a cell that has its nucleus pushed off to the very edge.

524
00:31:41,940 --> 00:31:46,740
And what's pushing off the nucleus to the very edge is the production of mucus by the tumor

525
00:31:46,740 --> 00:31:50,820
cells or mucin by the tumor cells, which often sits in a little circular space.

526
00:31:51,260 --> 00:31:52,240
So that's the first point.

527
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:53,320
That's a signet ring cell.

528
00:31:53,740 --> 00:31:57,840
Now, in the diffuse type, these signet ring cells, they diffusely infiltrate the wall of

529
00:31:57,840 --> 00:31:58,240
the stomach.

530
00:31:58,680 --> 00:32:00,960
And that results in something called desmoplasia.

531
00:32:01,060 --> 00:32:02,080
What's desmoplasia?

532
00:32:02,720 --> 00:32:05,860
Desmoplasia is a reactive response of the stroma.

533
00:32:05,860 --> 00:32:11,700
So it's just basically fibrous tissue in some blood vessels that occurs in response to the

534
00:32:11,700 --> 00:32:12,540
invasive tumor.

535
00:32:13,000 --> 00:32:17,480
And so this desmoplasia then results in thickening of the stomach wall so that these patients get

536
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:18,520
linitis plastica.

537
00:32:18,940 --> 00:32:21,800
Now, important to note, this is not associated with H. pylori.

538
00:32:22,360 --> 00:32:24,720
It's not associated with intestinal metoplasia.

539
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:27,220
And it's not associated with nitrosamines.

540
00:32:27,860 --> 00:32:32,260
Here's a picture of the diffuse type in which you have thickening of the stomach wall with

541
00:32:32,260 --> 00:32:35,760
this white fibrous kind of reaction called desmoplasia.

542
00:32:36,160 --> 00:32:38,780
Were you to do a biopsy, you would see these signet ring cells.

543
00:32:38,900 --> 00:32:39,920
Here's a classic cell.

544
00:32:40,020 --> 00:32:41,200
Here's a classic signet ring cell.

545
00:32:41,440 --> 00:32:45,880
You can see that there's a circular mucus, and it's pushed off the nucleus to one edge

546
00:32:45,880 --> 00:32:46,420
of the cell.

547
00:32:46,920 --> 00:32:51,440
And so this is a very important feature to be aware of, and that is that in the diffuse

548
00:32:51,440 --> 00:32:53,220
type, you get signet ring cells.

549
00:32:53,520 --> 00:32:55,120
So let me just summarize very briefly.

550
00:32:55,300 --> 00:32:57,100
There's two types of stomach cancer.

551
00:32:57,100 --> 00:33:00,120
There's an intestinal type, which produces an ulcer.

552
00:33:00,700 --> 00:33:05,620
And then there is a diffuse type, which diffusely thickens the wall of the stomach, on gross

553
00:33:05,620 --> 00:33:08,320
exam, and is characterized by signet ring cells.

554
00:33:08,980 --> 00:33:12,960
Now, gastric carcinoma, whether it be the diffuse type or whether it be the intestinal type,

555
00:33:13,020 --> 00:33:13,960
it presents late.

556
00:33:14,460 --> 00:33:19,900
The classic signs are going to be weight loss, abdominal pain, anemia, and they get early

557
00:33:19,900 --> 00:33:20,440
satiety.

558
00:33:20,940 --> 00:33:26,480
And early satiety would be classic in the diffuse type because in that particular case, you've

559
00:33:26,480 --> 00:33:29,260
got this thickening and fibrosis of the wall.

560
00:33:29,260 --> 00:33:33,720
And so the stomach can't expand, and the patients would feel full with a little bit of food.

561
00:33:33,780 --> 00:33:34,960
That's called early satiety.

562
00:33:35,480 --> 00:33:39,100
Now, there are some interesting signs that can be associated with gastric carcinoma.

563
00:33:39,420 --> 00:33:44,900
For example, one thing that can be seen with gastric carcinoma is something called acanthrosis

564
00:33:44,900 --> 00:33:50,180
nigricans, where you get a thickening and darkening of the skin, in particular in the axillary region.

565
00:33:50,340 --> 00:33:52,980
So that can be a sign that the patient has gastric carcinoma.

566
00:33:53,360 --> 00:33:56,420
Another very interesting sign is called the lesser trelot sign.

567
00:33:56,420 --> 00:34:01,560
And this is where the patients get dozens of seborrheic keratosis all over their skin

568
00:34:01,560 --> 00:34:03,420
that just sort of arise all of a sudden.

569
00:34:03,800 --> 00:34:06,420
And this can also be seen with gastric carcinoma.

570
00:34:06,500 --> 00:34:07,980
And you really want to know that association.

571
00:34:08,140 --> 00:34:08,840
It's high yield.

572
00:34:09,440 --> 00:34:11,560
Now, gastric carcinoma will spread to the lymph nodes.

573
00:34:11,660 --> 00:34:12,220
It's a cancer.

574
00:34:12,560 --> 00:34:17,760
And one of the interesting lymph nodes that it can often spread to is the left supraclavicular

575
00:34:17,760 --> 00:34:18,060
node.

576
00:34:18,100 --> 00:34:19,240
And that's called Verkau's node.

577
00:34:19,300 --> 00:34:21,780
And that's one of the nodes that drains the stomach.

578
00:34:21,780 --> 00:34:28,060
So the left supraclavicular node is important to be aware of in the context of gastric carcinoma.

579
00:34:28,920 --> 00:34:32,340
When you get distant metastases, usually it involves the liver.

580
00:34:33,240 --> 00:34:35,540
However, there are some classic sites to be aware of.

581
00:34:35,900 --> 00:34:37,720
One is the peri-umbilical region.

582
00:34:38,080 --> 00:34:41,940
And so we can get metastases of stomach cancer to the peri-umbilical region.

583
00:34:42,120 --> 00:34:44,720
That's called the Sister Mary Joseph nodule.

584
00:34:45,160 --> 00:34:49,120
And that's classically seen with the intestinal type of gastric cancer.

585
00:34:49,120 --> 00:34:54,000
Another interesting place that you can have metastasis to is the bilateral ovaries.

586
00:34:54,380 --> 00:34:59,880
And when you get bilateral ovary involvement by gastric cancer, that's called a Kruckenberg

587
00:34:59,880 --> 00:35:00,180
tumor.

588
00:35:00,560 --> 00:35:03,400
And again, that's classically seen with the diffuse type.

589
00:35:03,720 --> 00:35:05,520
Again, the diffuse type has signet ring cells.

590
00:35:05,880 --> 00:35:07,640
Those signet ring cells make mucus.

591
00:35:08,180 --> 00:35:13,840
And remember that a Kruckenberg tumor is basically defined as bilateral mucinous carcinoma

592
00:35:13,840 --> 00:35:16,880
involving the ovary as a metastatic phenomenon.

593
00:35:16,880 --> 00:35:21,840
And so that's an important association, diffuse type resulting in Kruckenberg tumor.

594
00:35:22,400 --> 00:35:23,920
And that's the end of our stomach section.

595
00:35:24,020 --> 00:35:25,220
The next section is small bowel.

